  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 117 PM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nSurface high pressure will build across the high plains through\nSaturday morning, with brisk north winds at 15-30 mph. There \nmay be a lull in the winds this evening with the loss of daytime\nmixing. Highs in the 50s look on track for Friday. As surface \nhigh pressure settles across the high plains Friday night, clear\nskies and light winds will allow for strong radiational \ncooling, with temperatures falling into the 20s in far western \nKansas and lower 30s in south central Kansas. Thus, a freeze \nwatch was issued for all of central and southwest Kansas with \nthe exception of Barber County.\n\nSurface high pressure is forecast to slowly move off to the east\nthis weekend, with a warming trend expected; and this warmup \nwill persist next week. A weak upper level trough is predicted \nto approach the high plains by early Monday; but given the lack \nof low to mid level moisture, rain is not expected. Highs are \nforecast to reach near or above 80 by the Tuesday to Thursday \ntime frame. There will be a very slow return of moisture next \nweek and into next weekend as a strong upper level trough \namplifies over the western USA, resulting in lee troughing and \nsouth winds. However, the rich moisture return will not occur \nuntil the upper level trough gets closer. Rain chances increase \nby next Friday and Saturday as the trough approaches the high \nplains. But even then there is uncertainty in how much rich \nmoisture will make it into western Kansas. Given that the \nsouthern and central plains will be in the warm sector, with no \nsource for cold air initially, it remains to be seen if enough \npotential instability will be present for thunderstorms. If the \nsystem slows down and colder air does progress southward ahead \nof it, then rain chances would be higher; but this is a low \nprobability scenario. Bottom line is that the conservative route\non rain chances seems appropriate given the uncertainty. And we\nare getting to that dry time of year when a special upper level\npattern is required for widespread rain on the high plains. This\nis due to the stronger westerly momentum across the Rockies \nthat tends to shunt moisture to the east of here. Probability \nwise, the grand ensemble indicates only 10-25% chances for .5\" \nor greater of rain by next Friday.",
